{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_4>\nimport re\n\nn = input()\n\n# Replace first 'at' (not at start) with '@'\ndef replace_first_at(s):\n    match = re.search(r'(?<=.at)', s)\n    if match:\n        pos = match.start() - 1\n        return s[:pos] + '@' + s[pos+2:]\n    return s\n\nn = replace_first_at(n)\n\n# Replace all 'dot' (not at start) with '.'\ndef replace_all_dots(s):\n    while True:\n        match = re.search(r'(?<=.dot)', s)\n        if not match:\n            break\n        pos = match.start() - 1\n        if pos < 1 or pos > len(s) - 3:\n            break\n        s = s[:pos] + '.' + s[pos+3:]\n    return s\n\nn = replace_all_dots(n)\n\nprint(n)\n</your_rewritten_code_4>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport re\n\ns = input().strip()\n\n# We need to find exactly one '@' and any number of '.' in the string\n# The string is made of \"dot\" and \"at\" representations\n\n# Strategy: \n# 1. Find all occurrences of \"at\" and \"dot\" and consider replacements\n# 2. Since we want minimal length and lexicographically smallest, we should replace \"at\" with '@' and \"dot\" with '.'\n# 3. But we must ensure the result is a valid email: exactly one '@', no leading/trailing '.', no leading/trailing '@'\n\n# We'll try to find the first valid \"at\" that can be the @ symbol (not at start or end)\n# Then replace all valid \"dot\" occurrences (not at start or end of the whole string, and not adjacent to @)\n\n# First, let's find all positions of \"at\" and \"dot\"\ndef find_all_occurrences(text, pattern):\n    positions = []\n    start = 0\n    while True:\n        pos = text.find(pattern, start)\n        if pos == -1:\n            break\n        positions.append(pos)\n        start = pos + 1\n    return positions\n\nat_positions = find_all_occurrences(s, \"at\")\ndot_positions = find_all_occurrences(s, \"dot\")\n\n# We need exactly one '@', so we'll try each \"at\" as the potential @ position\nbest_result = None\n\nfor at_pos in at_positions:\n    # Check if this \"at\" can be the @ symbol (not at start or end of entire string)\n    if at_pos == 0 or at_pos + 2 >= len(s):\n        continue\n    \n    # Build the email by replacing this \"at\" with '@'\n    email = s[:at_pos] + '@' + s[at_pos+2:]\n    \n    # Now replace \"dot\" with '.' but be careful about positions\n    # We need to adjust dot positions since we removed 2 characters for \"at\"\n    # Also, we need to avoid replacing dots that would make email start/end with '.' or have consecutive dots issues\n    \n    # Find all dot positions in the original string that are still valid in the new string\n    new_dot_positions = []\n    for dp in dot_positions:\n        if dp < at_pos:\n            new_dot_positions.append(dp)\n        elif dp >= at_pos + 2:\n            new_dot_positions.append(dp - 2)\n    \n    # Try replacing dots that are not at start/end and not adjacent to @\n    # We'll replace all valid dots (not at start/end of email, and not immediately before/after @)\n    result = email\n    # Process dots from end to start to maintain positions\n    for dp in sorted(new_dot_positions, reverse=True):\n        if dp == 0 or dp == len(result) - 1:\n            continue\n        if dp > 0 and result[dp-1] == '@':\n            continue\n        if dp < len(result) - 1 and result[dp+1] == '@':\n            continue\n        if dp > 0 and result[dp-1] == '.':\n            continue\n        if dp < len(result) - 1 and result[dp+1] == '.':\n            continue\n        # Check if this dot is part of \"dot\" in original string\n        # We need to verify that at position dp in the current result, it corresponds to a \"dot\" in original\n        # Actually, since we're processing based on original positions, we should be okay\n        \n        # Replace \"dot\" with '.' at position dp\n        if dp >= 2 and dp + 2 < len(result) and result[dp-2:dp+3] == \"dot\":\n            result = result[:dp-2] + '.' + result[dp+3:]\n        elif dp >= 1 and dp + 2 < len(result) and result[dp-1:dp+3] == \"dot\":\n            result = result[:dp-1] + '.' + result[dp+3:]\n        elif dp + 2 < len(result) and result[dp:dp+3] == \"dot\":\n            result = result[:dp] + '.' + result[dp+3:]\n    \n    # Validate the result\n    if '@' not in result:\n        continue\n    at_count = result.count('@')\n    if at_count != 1:\n        continue\n    \n    if result[0] == '.' or result[-1] == '.':\n        continue\n    if result[0] == '@' or result[-1] == '@':\n        continue\n    \n    # Check for consecutive dots or other invalid patterns\n    if '..' in result:\n        continue\n    \n    # Check that all characters are valid\n    if not all(c in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz.@' for c in result):\n        continue\n    \n    # Check that there's at least one character before and after @\n    at_idx = result.index('@')\n    if at_idx == 0 or at_idx == len(result) - 1:\n        continue\n    \n    # Check that there's at least one character between @ and the last dot (if any)\n    domain_part = result[at_idx+1:]\n    if '.' in domain_part:\n        last_dot = domain_part.rfind('.')\n        if last_dot == 0 or last_dot == len(domain_part) - 1:\n            continue\n    \n    # Valid email found\n    if best_result is None or len(result) < len(best_result) or (len(result) == len(best_result) and result < best_result):\n        best_result = result\n\nif best_result is None:\n    # Fallback: try to replace all \"at\" except the first one, and all \"dot\"\n    # This shouldn't happen for valid inputs, but just in case\n    s = s.replace(\"at\", \"@\", 1)\n    s = s.replace(\"dot\", \".\")\n    best_result = s\n\nprint(best_result)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -0.0011185110779479146, -1.311301275563892e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.18042506277561188, -0.0007286992622539401, -1.8596476365928538e-05, -0.3946228623390198, -1.19209